Also carries two changes unrelated to projection hosting, swept in by a
whole-tree add while they were in flight:

Noise moved from a carrier to a session layer. It had been NoiseTransport,
an `impl Transport` over TCP sitting beside iroh as a third way to move
bytes, which contradicts this tree's own plane split (iroh is the byte
plane). Noise is a handshake framework with no opinion about what carries
it, so it now composes over a stream the carrier already opened:
`noise::handshake` / `secure_initiator` / `secure_responder`, generic over
any AsyncRead + AsyncWrite. The TCP half survives as NoiseListener, the
standalone case, deliberately not a Transport. The old `impl` had a
`connect` that always failed, which was the tell.

That also made the layered identity real. The Noise static had derived
from the persona master, the same key every carrier binds with, so both
traced to one root and nothing was layered. The identity is now a
parameter of the handshake. Receipt:
noise_over_an_iroh_stream_layers_a_second_identity, over a live iroh
connection.

Knot's dial-hint refresh loop, closing R1's remaining half. The schema
could store a hint but nothing wrote one. KnotSyncHost gained known_peers
and peer_ticket; refresh_dial_hints rides the existing 5-second pairing
poll. Connected peers only, write only on change, reload-modify-save
because --pair-writer is a second writer to the file.

Both are described in
design_docs/mere_docs/implementation_strategy/2026-08-03_reachability_rungs_and_privacy_lanes_plan.md
